---

## Weaknesses

**1. No computational cost analysis despite heavy inference demands.** The paper demonstrates that increasing world-model inference count per episode improves task success rates, but provides no wall-clock time, FPS, or GPU-hour measurements for any of the benchmarked models. This omission is particularly significant given that models like Wan2.1 (14B) and Wan2.2-A14B are invoked multiple times per decision step, and inference-time scaling is presented as a key finding. Without cost reporting, it is impossible for practitioners to assess whether the demonstrated gains are practically viable.

**2. All evaluation is confined to simulation; no real-world or hardware transfer is discussed.** Every task—Active Recognition, Image-Goal Navigation, A-EQA, and Robotic Manipulation—is evaluated exclusively in Habitat-Sim or CoppeliaSim. For a benchmark paper claiming that world models "endow embodied agents with predictive perception for decision making," the complete absence of real-world experiments or even a sim-to-real gap analysis significantly limits the generalizability of the findings to actual embodied AI deployments.

**3. The data scaling law claim is insufficiently supported.** The paper claims to present "the first data scaling law for world models in embodied settings," but this is derived from only three models (Wan2.2†, Wan2.1†, SVD†) across four training-set sizes (400–80K instances) evaluated on a single task (Active Recognition). A proper scaling law typically requires systematic variation across model sizes and compute budgets with power-law fitting. The presented evidence is better characterized as a learning curve than a scaling law, and generalizing it across model families and tasks is not justified.

**4. Statistical significance is not reported for small episode counts.** Evaluation sets are relatively small—144 episodes for ImageNav, 184 for A-EQA, and 50 episodes per task for Manipulation—yet no error bars, confidence intervals, or significance tests are provided. Many of the reported differences between models fall in the 1–3 percentage point range in success rate, making it unclear whether the differences are meaningful rather than due to random variance across episodes.

**5. Robotic manipulation results show marginal and inconsistent gains.** Despite being one of four tasks presented as demonstrating comprehensive coverage, post-trained video generators in manipulation achieve only ~2 percentage point improvements over the VLM baseline (e.g., SVD† at 46.5% vs. baseline 44.5%), and even Cosmos-P2† underperforms the baseline (45.0% vs. 44.5%). The paper attributes this to the difficulty of modeling contact-rich dynamics, but this effectively means the benchmark's most action-critical domain yields inconclusive results, weakening the claim of broad utility across diverse embodied tasks.

**6. Inclusion of a proprietary, non-reproducible model creates an uneven evaluation.**  Runway Gen4 is benchmarked on Active Recognition and achieves the best zero-shot performance (64.79% SR) but cannot be post-trained, accessed, or reproduced by the research community. Including it alongside open-source models without analyzing what architectural or training properties drive its advantage makes it difficult to derive actionable insights from the comparison, and it cannot serve as a fair reference for future work.

**7. The contribution of the world model is not cleanly disentangled from the proposal and revision policies.** The unified planning framework involves a proposal policy (e.g., a VLM), a world model for simulation, and a revision policy. Performance gains reported as "world model utility" reflect the joint system rather than the world model in isolation. Since the revision policy alone—even using a simple LPIPS score without a world model—can substantially improve navigation (Table 5 shows the LPIPS revision policy raises SVD†'s SR from 43.05% to 47.92%), the incremental contribution of world-model rollouts specifically is not cleanly isolated, complicating interpretation of the main findings.
