---

**Weaknesses**

1. **Single hardware platform limits generalizability of energy claims.** All experiments are conducted on a single Intel i7-1255U laptop. The RAPL energy measurement infrastructure is specific to Intel x86 CPUs and is unavailable on ARM processors, AMD CPUs (with different RAPL domain semantics), or any GPU-based system. Given that ML workloads predominantly run on server-class CPUs and GPUs—neither of which is tested—the paper's headline claims of 25–50% energy savings cannot be taken as broadly representative. The measurement setup also requires disabling CPU security features and fixing the CPU frequency to a constant value, conditions that diverge sharply from realistic deployment environments.

2. **GPU evaluation is relegated to an appendix with no integration into the main analysis.** The paper acknowledges GPU results exist (Appendix F) but does not incorporate them into the core evaluation. In modern ML pipelines, sampling operations are frequently performed on GPUs as part of training or inference loops (e.g., in VAEs or diffusion models). GPU memory access patterns, parallelism, and energy characteristics differ fundamentally from the CPU model underlying cLUT's design. Excluding this from the main evaluation leaves open whether the method's advantages transfer to the hardware where ML sampling is most frequently performed.

3. **Benchmarks rely exclusively on synthetically generated exponential distributions.** The paper evaluates on distributions drawn from exponential distributions with varying parameters across a fixed size range. Real ML distributions—such as empirical token distributions in language models, graph neighborhood distributions in GNNs, or categorical distributions in Bayesian inference—often exhibit very different structural properties (e.g., power-law tails, near-uniform distributions, or highly dynamic supports). The extent to which cLUT's compression ratios and speed advantages hold for such distributions is not empirically verified.

4. **The 10–100× speedup comparison against Python libraries conflates algorithmic and implementation advantages.** The dominant performance gain when comparing cLUT against NumPy, PyTorch, and JAX arises because cLUT is implemented in compiled C while the baselines are called through Python interpreters. This conflates the algorithmic contribution with a language-level engineering choice. A fair algorithmic comparison would either also implement baselines in C (as is done for the SOTA C comparison in a separate experiment) or use an optimized Python extension of cLUT and compare against the libraries' underlying C/C++ implementations directly.

5. **Only one real-world ML application is demonstrated.** Despite motivating cLUT through diverse ML applications—variational autoencoders, diffusion models, contrastive learning with negative sampling, Bayesian deep learning—the paper demonstrates end-to-end impact in only a single use case (TrueSkill). It is not shown whether the preprocessing overhead remains acceptable when the sampled distribution changes frequently (as in online or adaptive learning settings), or whether cLUT's break-even point is achievable in practice for the ML workflows invoked in the introduction.

6. **The preprocessing cost analysis is insufficient for dynamic ML settings.** The break-even analysis assumes that a fixed distribution is sampled many times after a one-time preprocessing phase. In many ML training scenarios—negative sampling in contrastive learning, dynamic graph sampling, or evolving posterior approximations in Bayesian models—the underlying distribution changes at every training step. In such settings, the preprocessing overhead may never be amortized, and cLUT's advantage over methods with cheaper setup costs (e.g., the Alias method) may be eliminated. This limitation is not discussed.
